<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runbooks"
      toc={[
        {"model", "The execution model"},
        {"authoring", "Authoring and versions"},
        {"inputs", "Inputs and bindings"},
        {"outputs", "Outputs, success, and waits"},
        {"packs", "Pack resolution"},
        {"approvals", "Approvals and failure"},
        {"examples", "Production patterns"},
        {"results", "Results for people and agents"},
        {"limits", "Limits"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runbooks">
        <:dek>
          Turn a reviewed operational procedure into typed, staged action dispatches. Every target,
          runner, action, argument, output check, approval decision, and terminal result is frozen
          and audited.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/runbooks.webp"
        preview_h="max-h-[28rem]"
        title="emisar console · Runbooks"
        caption="The structured editor makes stage barriers, actions, targets, bindings, outputs, conditions, and retry policy explicit."
        alt="The emisar runbook editor showing typed inputs, staged actions, and an exact publish review."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="model">The execution model</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runbook contains ordered <strong class="text-zinc-200">stages</strong>. A stage contains
        one or more <strong class="text-zinc-200">steps</strong>, and each step names one declared
        catalog action, its pack, typed argument bindings, and runner or group targets. A group
        target becomes one logical item per matching runner when execution begins.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Stages are barriers.</strong> The next stage starts only
            after every item in the current stage succeeds.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Sequential mode</strong> completes one step's complete
            fan-out before starting the next step. Use it when order matters between different
            actions.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Parallel mode</strong>
            makes the stage's items eligible
            together, while
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">max_parallel</code>
            caps active attempts. Set it to
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">1</code>
            for one-host-at-a-time fleet maintenance.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="One action is still the execution boundary.">
        A runner receives one declared pack action at a time. Runbooks coordinate those actions in
        the control plane; they do not add a shell, script, HTTP, or code escape hatch.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="authoring">Authoring and versions</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open
        <.link navigate="/app/runbooks" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Runbooks
        </.link>
        and choose <.link navigate="/app/runbooks/new" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          New runbook
        </.link>.
        If you already have Definition v1 JSON, choose
        <.link navigate="/app/runbooks/import" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Import runbook
        </.link>
        to select or drop a file, or paste the document directly. Imports start as drafts. The
        structured editor covers every
        field in the runbook contract: typed inputs, stages, concurrency, actions, targets,
        bindings, outputs, conditions, and retry policy. It validates continuously and shows the
        exact current runner, action, and pack plan before publishing. An invalid or unresolved
        draft can be saved, but it cannot be published. There is no second raw-definition editor to
        drift from the form.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Drafts are reviewable, not runnable.</strong> Saving a
            change creates a new draft version. Publishing freezes that version under <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">slug@version</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Context travels with the procedure.</strong> Add
            prerequisites, stop conditions, ownership, and expected outcomes using headings,
            paragraphs, lists, and fenced code. Links, images, and raw HTML stay inert text.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">JSON is the canonical interchange format.</strong>
            The
            console, persistence layer, and MCP use the same strict
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">schema_version: 1</code>
            definition. Unknown fields and old flat step lists are rejected.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="inputs">Inputs and bindings</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Declare a run-time input as a string, integer, number, boolean, or enum. Inputs can be
        required, constrained by a range or length, given a default, and marked sensitive.
        Sensitive values are
        accepted for dispatch but replaced with
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">[REDACTED]</code>
        in plans and results; sensitive inputs cannot carry stored defaults.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every action argument uses exactly one whole-value binding:
        a JSON literal, a declared runbook input, or a named output from an earlier stage. The
        compiler checks the binding against the trusted action argument schema before any execution
        row is created. It also rejects ambiguous fan-out: a later item may consume one unique
        producer output, or the output from the same runner when both stages fan out over that
        runner.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="outputs">Outputs, success, and waits</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An action may return more than the procedure needs. Name only the values the runbook should
        retain. Extract them from structured JSON with a JSON Pointer, or from complete
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">stdout</code>
        or <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">stderr</code>
        using contains, literal grep, or a bounded regular expression capture. Text extraction
        fails closed if the stream is missing, truncated, invalid UTF-8, or over its safety budget.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Success is explicit.</strong> Conditions compare named
            extracted values with equality, ordering, membership, containment, or a regular
            expression. Every configured condition must pass.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Waits repeat observations only.</strong> If an action
            succeeds but its conditions are not met, a wait can run that same action again after a
            fixed interval until the timeout or attempt cap. Action failure, denial, refusal, or
            extraction failure is never retried by a wait.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Evidence survives.</strong> Extracted values, their
            integrity digest, condition outcomes, attempts, and wait state remain attached to the
            logical item and its audit events.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="packs">Pack resolution</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A step has one <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">pack</code>
        object containing its ID. The target selection comes first in the editor, so the action
        menu includes only actions currently available across those runners. Preflight resolves
        the current trusted pack behind that action, requires one compatible action contract across
        the selection, then freezes each exact <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">pack@version/sha256:hash</code>.
        A later pack update cannot reinterpret an in-flight execution. If the current catalog no
        longer provides a compatible action, the runbook is blocked until you edit and publish a
        working version.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="approvals">Approvals and failure</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Runbooks do not define another approval policy. The account policy evaluates every item in
        the frozen plan. If any item requires approval, emisar opens one request for the complete
        runbook execution: every stage, action, target, exact pack, argument, and policy decision
        appears in that request. No action run exists before the request is approved.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Approval applies only to that exact frozen execution. Approver scope must cover every
        runner. Before approval and before each physical attempt, emisar rechecks current
        membership, runner access, policy, pack trust, action contract, and runner state. A current
        denial or incompatible change stops the execution; approval never overrides policy drift.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A denial, refusal, timeout, cancellation, action failure, failed extraction, unmet terminal
        condition, lost authorization, or unusable frozen pack halts the execution. Already-running
        peers may report their real outcomes, but no new item and no later stage starts. Cancelling
        an execution also cancels pending stages, waits, and active attempts without erasing their
        history.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="examples">Production patterns</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This minimal definition is valid Definition v1 JSON. The editor can build the larger
        production patterns described below without introducing another authoring format.
      </p>
      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-100">PostgreSQL replication gate</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Fan out the catalog's read-only replication check, require a streaming replica, and wait
        within a fixed observation budget when the first result is not ready.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Definition v1 JSON">{~s({
  "schema_version": 1,
  "context_markdown": "Confirm the incident and current leader before proceeding.",
  "inputs": [],
  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "preflight",
      "title": "Inspect replication",
      "mode": "parallel",
      "max_parallel": 4,
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "replication_lag",
          "pack": {"id": "postgres"},
          "action": "postgres.replication_lag",
          "targets": {"refs": ["group:data-postgres"]},
          "args": {},
          "outputs": [
            {
              "id": "has_streaming_replica",
              "source": "stdout",
              "sensitive": false,
              "extract": {"type": "contains", "expression": "|streaming|"}
            }
          ],
          "success": [
            {"output": "has_streaming_replica", "operator": "equals", "value": true}
          ],
          "wait": {
            "interval_seconds": 15,
            "timeout_seconds": 300,
            "max_attempts": 20
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
})}</.docs_code>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-100">One-host-at-a-time maintenance</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Inspect all workers in parallel, maintain one worker at a time, and start fleet-wide
        verification only after every host succeeds. If policy requires approval for the
        maintenance action, the operator approves the complete frozen execution once.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">1. Inspect:</strong> parallel reads across the fleet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">2. Maintain:</strong>
          one parallel stage with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">max_parallel: 1</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">3. Verify:</strong> fleet-wide checks after the barrier.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="Use composite actions at the right boundary.">
        If each host must drain, mutate, and confirm local recovery before the next host starts,
        expose that as one independently useful pack action. A sequential runbook step completes its
        complete fan-out; it does not interleave separate steps per runner.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="results">Results for people and agents</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The execution page keeps the operator context above a stage-and-item matrix. Each item shows
        its target, exact pack and hash, action, attempts, wait state, extracted outputs, condition
        evidence, and a stable error when it did not succeed. Sensitive values stay redacted.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Connected agents use <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">list_runbooks</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">get_runbook</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">execute_runbook</code>,
        and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">create_runbook_draft</code>.
        They receive the same canonical definition and structured stage/item result, with a
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">blocking</code>
        reason and exact <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">wait_for_run</code>
        continuation while work remains. Draft creation never publishes or executes.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Runbooks currently fail closed on a signature-enforcing runner with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">signed_runbook_unsupported</code>.
        The MCP bridge signs exact direct
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">run_action</code>
        calls, not an expanded multi-stage plan.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="limits">Limits</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        One definition is at most 64 KiB and contains up to 32 inputs, 16 stages, 32 steps in total,
        16 target refs per step, and 256 resolved logical items. A stage may run 1 through 16
        attempts concurrently when it uses parallel mode. Each step may retain 16 outputs and
        evaluate 16 conditions. An execution, including waits, ends within 24 hours. The
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/limits"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          limits reference
        </.link>
        covers the wider API and retention caps.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="What belongs in a runbook?">
        Use one for a reviewed diagnostic or remediation sequence whose stages, stopping rules, and
        expected result are known. Keep open-ended investigation in the action catalog, where an
        operator or agent can inspect evidence and choose the next exact action.
      </.docs_callout>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
